## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by reference — a new reference every render means a new prop, even if the data is identical.

```jsx
// Every render creates a new object reference → child always re-renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Every render creates a new function reference → same problem
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

JavaScript allocates a new object/function literal on every call, so the reference changes even when the value doesn't.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props haven't changed (shallow compare).
Use it on pure components that receive stable props and are expensive to render.

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed *value*.
Use it when a calculation is expensive, or when you need a stable object reference passed as a prop.

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a *function*.
Use it when passing callbacks to memoized children or effect dependency arrays.

```
React.memo  →  stabilize the component
useMemo     →  stabilize a value / object
useCallback →  stabilize a function
```

Don't reach for these by default — the memoization itself has cost. Profile first.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context *value reference* changes. The classic mistake:

```jsx
// Bad: new object every render → all consumers re-render on every parent render
<AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, login, logout }}>
```

Fix: memoize the value object.

```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, login, logout }), [user, login, logout]);
<AuthContext.Provider value={value}>
```

A second pitfall: one large context with mixed concerns. A frequent write (e.g. mouse position) in the same context as rarely-changing data (e.g. user role) triggers all consumers on every mouse move. Split contexts by update frequency.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke because `filters` and `onSelect` are recreated each render:

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);

  const filters = { query, active: true };           // new object every render
  const handleSelect = (item) => setSelected(item);  // new function every render

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = ({ filters, onSelect }) => { /* ... */ };
// No memo → re-renders unconditionally
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `query` or `selected` actually changes:

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);

  const filters = useMemo(() => ({ query, active: true }), [query]);
  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => setSelected(item), []);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(({ filters, onSelect }) => { /* ... */ });
```

The three pieces work together: `React.memo` makes the component opt into shallow comparison, `useMemo`/`useCallback` make the props stable enough to pass that comparison.

---

**Rule of thumb:** measure with React DevTools Profiler before adding memoization. The most common real-world win is `useCallback` on handlers passed to long lists wrapped in `React.memo`.
